Title: The Roof
Order: 5
Production Code: 1x05

Cast: JIM DAVIS, JULIA, THE NARRATOR. Running time target: 10-13 minutes.

PRODUCTION NOTE: Twilight to night. The mix turns from the emptiness of the day to the open sky: wind, distance, high air. The only intimacy in the series; keep both voices close and warm. Music is allowed to speak for the first time since 1x01.

EXT. EAST SIDE - DUSK

AMB: the river, softly; the light fading; a faint, cooling breeze

SFX: the Stutz's engine starting; Jim's hands on the wheel; he is driving

NARRATOR
Upward they turned toward life again, and he seized the wheel.

SFX: the car pulling out; the gear shifts, deft, deliberate

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
The world was darkening to twilight, and a great, gray pall was falling, mercifully and gently, on the sleeping dead.

MUSIC: BED IN - a muted horn, then strings; the first real melody since the morning, slow, tender, shadowed

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
The ghastly glare of reality seemed replaced with the dream of some vast romance.

SFX: the engine, a steady purr; the wind passing the open car

SFX: Julia's body settling back into the seat; silk, a long, slow sigh

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
The girl lay silently back, as the motor whizzed along, and looked half-consciously for the elf-queen to wave life into this dead world again.

JULIA (V.O.)
(dreamy, half-awake)
The elf-queen. She would come soon.

NARRATOR
She forgot to wonder at the quickness with which he had learned to drive her car. It seemed natural.

SFX: the car whirling and swinging into Madison Square; tires on gravel

MUSIC: SWELL - a single chord, bright

SFX: the car stopping at the door of the Metropolitan Tower; the brakes; a hush

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
And then as they whirled and swung into Madison Square, and at the door of the Metropolitan Tower, she gave a low cry, and her eyes were great.

SFX: Julia's sharp, low intake of breath; a little cry

JULIA
(in a whisper, to herself)
Perhaps she had seen the elf-queen?

MUSIC: BED OUT

INT. THE METROPOLITAN TOWER - ELEVATOR

SFX: the gate, folding; the cab humming upward

NARRATOR
The man led her to the elevator of the tower, and deftly they ascended.

SFX: floors clicking by; a brass indicator; the faint squeak of leather as she leans on the wall

INT. J.B.H.'S PRIVATE OFFICE

AMB: the room at night; the mantel clock; the hum of a building at rest

SFX: rugs dragged from corners; chair legs; cushions dropped; a lamp turned on and off again; a pen on paper

NARRATOR
In her father's office they gathered rugs and chairs, and he wrote a note, and laid it on the desk.

SFX: a sheet placed on the desk; weighted with a paperweight

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Then they ascended to the roof, and he made her comfortable.

SFX: a stair door; a long climb; a steel door pushed open; the wind rushing in

EXT. THE ROOF OF THE METROPOLITAN TOWER - NIGHT

AMB: wind at a great height; far below, the faint, sourceless sound of a city that is not making any; the flap of a loose awning

SFX: rugs spread; chairs set down; a shawl unfolded

NARRATOR
For a while she rested, and sank to dreamy somnolence, watching the worlds above, and wondering.

AMB: the wind drops to a whisper; one distant bell, once

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Below lay the dark shadows of the city, and afar was the shining of the sea.

SFX: Julia stirring; the gentle flutter of cloth; she looks up

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
She glanced at him timidly as he set food before her and took a shawl and wound her in it, touching her reverently, yet tenderly.

SFX: a plate set on a rug; a tin spoon; a shawl wound round her shoulders; a very quiet, rushed thank-you

JULIA
(softly)
Thank you.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
She looked up at him with thankfulness in her eyes, eating what he served.

SFX: eating, slowly; the clink of a spoon on a tin plate

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
He watched the city. She watched him. He seemed very human, very near now.

MUSIC: BED IN - strings alone, one voice, very quiet, like a thought she is not ready to say aloud

JULIA
(softly)
Have you had to work hard?

JIM DAVIS
(simply)
Always.

JULIA
(a beat)
I have always been idle. I was rich.

JIM DAVIS
(almost an echo)
I was poor.

JULIA
(slowly, a half-remembered line)
The rich and the poor are met together.

JIM DAVIS
(finishing it)
The Lord is the Maker of them all.

SFX: a long, slow breath; a shift of cloth

JULIA
(looking down over the dead city)
Yes. And how foolish our human distinctions seem - now.

JIM DAVIS
(low, bitter, then gentle)
Yes. I was not - human, yesterday.

SFX: she turns to look at him; a creak of a chair

JULIA
And your people were not my people. But today -

She does not finish.

NARRATOR
She paused. He was a man - no more. But he was in some larger sense a gentleman, sensitive, kindly, chivalrous, everything save his hands, and - his face. Yet yesterday -

JIM DAVIS
(a mutter)
Death, the leveler!

JULIA
(gently, rising to her feet)
And the revealer.

MUSIC: BED OUT

SFX: Jim turns away; fumbling with something on the roof; a box lid; a fuse

NARRATOR
He turned away, and after fumbling a moment, sent a rocket into the darkening air.

SFX: a long sizzle, a whoosh, a shriek as the rocket goes up; high, then breaking into stars with a series of small pops

AMB: the wind, taking the last of the sound

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
It arose, shrieked, and flew up, a slim path of light, and scattering its stars abroad, dropped on the city below.

SFX: the faintest crackle of sparks falling, a long way down

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
She scarcely noticed it. A vision of the world had risen before her.

MUSIC: BED IN - rising, slow, harp and strings, a theme that grows by single notes

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Slowly the mighty prophecy of her destiny overwhelmed her. Above the dead past hovered the Angel of Annunciation.

JULIA (V.O.)
(slowly, hushed)
I am no mere woman. I am neither high nor low, white nor black, rich nor poor.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
She was primal woman; mighty mother of all men to come, and Bride of Life.

MUSIC: SWELL - a wordless soprano line over the strings, brief, ecstatic

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
She looked upon the man beside her, and forgot all else but his manhood, his strong, vigorous manhood, his sorrow and sacrifice. She saw him glorified.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
He was no longer a thing apart, a creature below, a strange outcast of another clime and blood, but her Brother Humanity incarnate, Son of God and great All-Father of the race to be.

MUSIC: BED OUT - the soprano withdrawn; a single string remains

SFX: a rocket fuse hissing; another rocket whooshing up; a faint rain of sparks

NARRATOR
He did not glimpse the glory in her eyes, but stood looking outward toward the sea, and sending rocket after rocket into the unanswering darkness.

SFX: the rockets going up one after another; each one a shriek, a path of light, a pop

AMB: the wind lifting; a distant roll of wind through steel

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Dark-purple clouds lay banked and billowed in the west. Behind them and all around, the heavens glowed in dim, weird radiance, that suffused the darkening world and made almost a minor music.

MUSIC: BED IN - a minor key, a slow chord progression, strings and low winds, made of the sky itself

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Suddenly, as though gathered back in some vast hand, the great cloud-curtain fell away.

SFX: a sudden hush of wind; a sound like a great cloth being drawn aside

MUSIC: STING - a long, high, shining chord

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Low on the horizon lay a long, white star - mystic, wonderful! And from it fled upward to the pole, like some wan bridal veil, a pale, wide sheet of flame, that lighted all the world and dimmed the stars.

SFX: Jim's rocket falls from his fingers; it clatters on the roof, rolling

AMB: the whole sky seems to ring, a soft, high shimmer, like glass

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
In fascinated silence the man gazed at the heavens, and dropped his rockets to the floor.

SFX: more rockets falling; one by one, rolling on tar paper; then still

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Memories of memories stirred to life in the dead recesses of his mind. The shackles seemed to rattle and fall from his soul.

SFX: iron chains, far off, in the sound-mix; a rattle; then a clank as of chains falling to stone

MUSIC: BED IN - slow, low brass, steady, majestic; an Egyptian, Assyrian shape in the horns without any real instrument of those lands

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Up from the crass and crushing and cringing of his caste leaped the lone majesty of kings long dead.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
He arose within the shadows, tall, straight, and stern, with power in his eyes and ghostly scepters hovering to his grasp. It was as though some mighty Pharaoh lived again, or curled Assyrian lord.

SFX: a long, slow footstep on tar paper; Jim turning, drawing himself up

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
He turned and looked upon the lady, and found her gazing straight at him.

AMB: the wind drops; for the first time since morning, the city's silence feels like a held note

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Silently, immovably, they saw each other, face to face, eye to eye.

SFX: a very faint human breath, two of them, in unison

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Their souls lay naked to the night. It was not lust. It was not love. It was some vaster, mightier thing that needed neither touch of body nor thrill of soul. It was a thought divine, splendid.

MUSIC: SWELL - the soprano returns over the brass, wordless; the full mix opens for one bar

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Slowly, noiselessly, they moved toward each other - the heavens above, the seas around, the city grim and dead below.

SFX: two sets of steps across the roof; no word; silk, a heavier tread, closer

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
He loomed from out the velvet shadows, vast and dark. Pearl-white and slender, she shone beneath the stars.

SFX: a rustle of silk as she stretches out her jeweled hands; a faint chime of rings

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
She stretched her jeweled hands abroad. He lifted up his mighty arms, and they cried, each to the other, almost with one voice -

MUSIC: HOLD - the chord suspended

JIM DAVIS
(together with Julia, almost one voice)
The world is dead.

JULIA
(together with Jim)
The world is dead.

A beat. They are on the edge of the next line. She begins it:

JULIA (CONT'D)
Long live the -

MUSIC: CUT

SFX: a flat, hard, distant electric horn from the street below: HONK
